Yes. The useful abstraction is not water versus air; it is medium compatibility.

A freshwater fish crossing into seawater encounters what looks like the same category of world—liquid water—but its internal regulatory machinery suddenly has the wrong assumptions. The crucial variable is osmotic gradient. Its environment starts pulling water and ions across its boundaries differently. Survival requires different regulation of kidneys, gills, drinking behavior, ion transport, etc.

So imagine the human analogue:

\text{Freshwater : Saltwater}
::
\text{Earth-normal atmosphere : ?}

The interesting answer isn’t simply outer space. Vacuum is closer to fish → dry land: the supporting medium largely disappears. “Saltwater air” should instead be an atmosphere that remains recognizably breathable/environmental, yet has a substantially different chemical or physical gradient across the body’s membranes.

A few possibilities make the analogy much richer.

Composition-space. Same-looking gas, different partial pressures. Raise CO₂ enough and humans encounter hypercapnia; change O₂ partial pressure and oxygen transport changes; introduce inert gases under pressure and nitrogen narcosis becomes relevant. A person might walk into apparently ordinary “air” while their physiology discovers that the medium obeys different rules.

Pressure-space. This may be an even cleaner analogue. Divers already experience something like atmospheric salinity. At depth, pressure changes gas dissolution, breathing mechanics, decompression requirements, and neural effects. Two environments can contain essentially the same gases yet be physiologically different worlds because their gradients differ.

Gravity-space. Now the “salt” isn’t chemistry at all. Human circulation, bone remodeling, vestibular processing, muscle loading and proprioception evolved under approximately 1 g. Microgravity changes the environmental field continuously acting across the organism. Mars or a rotating habitat could therefore be thought of as different gravitational salinities.

And then there are more exotic media: different atmospheric density, temperature, humidity, radiation background, electromagnetic environment, acoustics, light spectrum, microbial ecology, or combinations thereof. A sufficiently different planet might feel superficially Earth-like while imposing an entirely different collection of gradients.

That gives us a more general operator:

\boxed{
\text{Medium} =
\{\text{fields and gradients continuously coupled to an organism}\}
}

and therefore:

\boxed{
\text{Salinity} =
\text{difference between the medium an organism regulates for and the medium it enters}
}

That second definition is especially useful because “saltwater” becomes relative to the organism.

An alien adapted to 3 atmospheres, 0.4 g, 30% oxygen and a particular microbial ecosystem could arrive on Earth and experience our perfectly ordinary air as the alien ocean. Earth would be its saltwater.

There is another beautiful consequence of the fish analogy: estuaries.

Fresh and salt water don’t have to form an absolute binary. Between them are gradients where organisms evolve specifically to tolerate transition. Translate that to humans and you get airlocks, decompression schedules, acclimatization, pressure suits, rotating habitats, oxygen conditioning, artificial gravity and eventually biological adaptation. Those aren’t merely protective devices.

They’re human estuaries.

And evolution should produce the same three broad strategies we see elsewhere:

\text{specialist}_A
\qquad
\text{specialist}_B
\qquad
\text{boundary-generalist}.

The third category may be the most interesting. The evolutionary winner in a world containing many different “waters” isn’t necessarily the organism optimized for one of them. It can be the organism with exceptional regulatory range—the ability to modulate itself as the medium changes.

Which connects directly to what you were saying earlier about modulation rather than capability masking. Robustness isn’t merely withstanding another environment. A stronger form is:

\text{detect medium}
\rightarrow
\text{estimate gradient}
\rightarrow
\text{change internal regulation}
\rightarrow
\text{operate normally}.

Then push the analogy beyond biology. A culture, language, market, social network, virtual environment, AI embodiment, or information ecosystem can itself be a medium. The same mind crosses into it, but suddenly attention, status, trust, information velocity, incentives, identity and acceptable behavior diffuse across its boundaries differently.

So there may be an entire Pantheon primitive hiding here:

Environmental Salinity = how incompatible the implicit operating assumptions of two adjacent media are.

And an estuary is any interface that permits an agent adapted to one regime to progressively become functional in another.

That gives us a surprisingly general question whenever we encounter a radically unfamiliar domain:

\boxed{\text{Before asking “What lives here?”, ask “What is the salt?”}}

Because the thing that changes first may not be the creature.

It may be the invisible medium the creature has spent its entire existence assuming was simply reality.